# Flash control plane (operator-side). Copy to .env and fill in.
# Provider credentials live ONLY here — clients never see them and authenticate
# with their API key.
#
# Everything under "required" is checked by `flash/providers/preflight.py` at
# startup; `flash-server` refuses to serve if any of them is missing. Settings
# that only DEGRADE the plane (a single-account RunPod pool, no GITHUB_TOKEN)
# warn at startup instead of blocking it.
#
# Self-hosting? Read SELF_HOSTING.md first - the short version is the two lines
# below, and it is a much smaller list than this file suggests:
#
#   FLASH_STANDALONE=1
#   FREESOLO_INTERNAL_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# IMPORTANT: `flash-server` does NOT read this file. It reads the process
# environment, so copying it to .env is not by itself enough — load it:
#
#   set -a && . ./.env && set +a && flash-server     # shell
#   docker run --env-file .env ...                   # docker

# --- required: GPU substrate (AT LEAST ONE of the three) ---
# Flash allocates across RunPod, Lambda, and Vast. Configure the ones you have an
# account with; a provider whose key is unset is simply never considered, and the
# allocator ranks candidates only on the substrates you configured. One is enough
# to run. The preflight fails only when ALL THREE are missing.
#
# RunPod: one key, or several comma-separated for multi-account failover. A
# single-account pool works but cannot fail over or reap across accounts, so the
# preflight warns.
RUNPOD_API_KEY=
# Lambda Cloud API key.
LAMBDA_API_KEY=
# Vast.ai API key.
VAST_API_KEY=

# --- required: artifacts ---
# HuggingFace token with write access to the dataset repos flash creates for artifacts
# (code upload + streamed checkpoints/adapters). The repo is assigned by the control
# plane and scoped to the ENVIRONMENT, so runs sharing an environment share a repo and
# each run lands under its own prefix. It is not a user-authored field: the TOML parser
# rejects a [train] hf_repo set by hand.
HF_TOKEN=
# The HuggingFace user or org those artifact repos are created under. Defaults to
# Freesolo's namespace, which a self-hoster's token cannot write to - so on a
# self-hosted plane SET THIS to a namespace your HF_TOKEN owns, or every run fails at
# the code upload before training starts.
# FLASH_HF_NAMESPACE=your-hf-username

# --- required: control-plane auth ---
# The plane's own auth key. On a self-hosted (FLASH_STANDALONE=1) deployment this is
# the ONLY credential the plane accepts and is therefore the entire trust boundary -
# generate a high-entropy value and treat it like a root password:
#
#   export FREESOLO_INTERNAL_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# On a managed deployment it is the shared internal key that maps to a single service
# identity without a network call, alongside per-user keys verified against
# {FREESOLO_BASE_URL}/api/auth/verify.
FREESOLO_INTERNAL_KEY=

# --- optional: self-hosting ---
# Run without a Freesolo backend. Off by default. With it set, the plane stops calling
# out for project/environment/billing validation and trusts FREESOLO_INTERNAL_KEY as a
# single-tenant operator credential; external bearer tokens are REJECTED rather than
# accepted unverified. Single tenant - see SELF_HOSTING.md before enabling.
# FLASH_STANDALONE=1

# --- optional ---
# Server token for the environment repository. Without it, environments hosted in a
# private GitHub repo cannot be fetched; environments in public GitHub repos still work,
# so this warns at startup rather than blocking it. (A local directory is never a valid
# environment source - the GPU worker fetches the environment itself.)
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# The OPD teacher credential (injected into the worker for opd jobs only). NOT covered
# by the startup preflight: an opd run submitted without it still allocates and BILLS a
# GPU, then fails inside the worker before the first teacher request. Set it before
# running opd; sft and grpo do not use it.
FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
# Where to verify user keys. Defaults to https://api.freesolo.co when unset. Ignored
# under FLASH_STANDALONE=1, which verifies nothing over the network.
# FREESOLO_BASE_URL=
# Adapter serving is delegated to a multi-LoRA serving app (a Modal app that serves
# every adapter on one GPU per base model, scaling to zero when idle).
# `flash deploy`/`undeploy`/`chat` register/deregister/chat against it. Defaults to the
# hosted URL when unset; point it at your own deployment to self-host serving.
#
# Under FLASH_STANDALONE=1 there is NO default: those three commands error out until this
# is set. Every serving request carries FREESOLO_INTERNAL_KEY, which on a self-hosted
# plane is the credential controlling that plane, so falling back to the hosted URL would
# send it to a service the operator does not run. Training never uses this.
#
# LEFT COMMENTED DELIBERATELY. The standalone guard rejects an UNSET value, so a template
# that assigned the hosted URL would satisfy it and hand the plane's root credential to
# serve.freesolo.co on the first deploy -- the exact leak the guard exists to stop. Set it
# to YOUR serving deployment; the managed plane gets the hosted URL from the code default.
# FREESOLO_SERVING_URL=https://serve.freesolo.co
